Time Complexity: O(N)

- Every node is visited exactly once.
- For each node, constant-time operations are performed (max, push, pop).
- Therefore, total time complexity is O(N).

Space Complexity: O(W) auxiliary space

Where W is the maximum width of the binary tree.

- Queue stores at most W nodes.
- ans stores one maximum value per level, requiring O(H) space.
- If counting the output vector, total space is O(W + H).
- Excluding the output vector, auxiliary space is O(W).

Final Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(N)

Auxiliary Space: O(W)

Output Space: O(H)

Where:

- N = Total number of nodes.
- W = Maximum width of the binary tree.
- H = Height of the binary tree.


/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode() : val(0), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x, TreeNode *left, TreeNode *right) : val(x), left(left), right(right) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> largestValues(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int>ans;
        if(root == NULL)
        {
            return ans;
        }
        queue<TreeNode*>q;
        q.push(root);
        while(!q.empty())
        {
            int sz = q.size();
            int maxi = INT_MIN;
            for(int i = 0; i < sz; i++)
            {
                TreeNode* node = q.front();
                q.pop();

                maxi = max(maxi,node->val);

                if(node->left)
                {
                    q.push(node->left);
                }
                if(node->right)
                {
                    q.push(node->right);
                }
            }
            ans.push_back(maxi);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
